  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "hi6jxjr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Sound is a mechanical longitudinal wave produced by vibrations and requires a medium to travel.",
        "Loudness is determined by amplitude (measured in decibels), whereas pitch is determined by frequency (measured in Hertz).",
        "Sound travels fastest in solids, followed by liquids, and slowest in gases; it cannot travel in a vacuum.",
        "An echo requires a minimum reflecting distance of 17 metres and a time gap of 0.1 second.",
        "SONAR stands for Sound Navigation And Ranging and uses ultrasonic waves for underwater applications."
      ],
      "id": "0dngi3r"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Speed in Air",
          "value": "343 m/s",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
